<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sultanate of Gowa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1300–<a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Gowa Sultanate, Kingdom of Gowa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sultanate of Gowa was a Makassarese state in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulawesi">Sulawesi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, centred on the port of Makassar. Its rulers adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in 1605 and spread it by alliance and war to neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a> lands and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumbawa">Sumbawa</a> and Bima. Gowa dominated the eastern spice trade until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> and its Bone allies defeated it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makassar-war">Makassar War</a>, ended by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bongaya">Treaty of Bongaya</a> in 1667.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-gowa.jpg" alt="Sultanate of Gowa" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Samhanin · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Sultanate_of_Gowa.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
